  "market_context": "During a strong prior bullish impulse (RSI surged from 18 to 89), price formed consecutive higher highs while RSI made lower highs. Early divergence entries were stopped out three times by liquidity grabs before the full setup confirmed.",
  "key_observation": "Price made higher highs while RSI made lower highs, but the valid short entry only occurred after RSI dropped below 50, price broke structure, retested the 21 MA, and completed a three-line strike pattern.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: The analyst identified a bearish regular divergence—price forming higher highs while RSI formed lower highs on the 15-minute chart.",
    "Step 2: He noted that early entries based solely on the first divergence were stopped out three times due to strong bullish momentum and liquidity grabs above swing highs.",
    "Step 3: He waited for confirmation: a break of market structure (lower high forming after prior higher highs) and price closing below the 21-period SMMA.",
    "Step 4: He observed a retest of the 21-period SMMA as dynamic resistance, with price rejecting downward from that level.",
    "Step 5: He confirmed bearish momentum by noting RSI had crossed and remained below the 50 level (not the traditional 70/30 bands).",
    "Step 6: He identified a three-line strike pattern—three consecutive bullish candles followed by a bearish engulfing candle—as the final confluence for entry.",
    "Step 7: He placed the short entry on the close of the engulfing candle, targeting the 200-period SMMA as the primary take-profit level."
  ],
  "outcome": "45 pips profit to the 200 MA target after entering on the three-line strike close; early divergence-only entries were stopped out three times.",
  "lessons": "Patience is critical: wait for multiple confluences (divergence + structure break + MA retest + RSI <50 + candlestick pattern) before entering; trading divergence alone leads to repeated stop-outs during strong momentum.",
